# Copy this file to `.env` beside it and fill in what you need. `.env` is in
# .gitignore and must never be committed.
#
# Nothing here is required to start. A hub with no .env at all runs: the admin
# panel opens (locked, and unlocking it is one button), and everything below can
# be pasted into that panel instead of typed here — it writes this same file.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. It is the whole of a hub's setup that is not the event
# itself, which makes it the whole of the backup: copy `.env` to a spare laptop,
# start the hub there, set the event key, and you have a second hub. Keep it off
# shared drives and out of screenshots; it is readable by anyone who has it.
#
# HOW LONG THE KEYS LAST. All of them last until you revoke them — there is no
# per-event key and nothing here expires between competitions. Set them once, at
# home, and the only thing that changes from one event to the next is the EVENT
# KEY in the admin panel. (The one thing that does expire is the Lovat browser
# token used to mint LOVAT_API_KEY — 72 hours — and that token is not stored
# here. The `lvt-` key it produces does not expire.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the hub

# The admin password for http://localhost:6059/ - the panel where the event
# key, the API keys and the mirror are set. Set it with:
#
#     python3 server/hub.py --set-admin-password
#
# which asks for it twice and writes the line below for you.
#
# The value is the password base64-encoded. THAT IS NOT ENCRYPTION: anybody who
# can read this file can decode it in one command. What it buys is that the
# password is not sitting in plain sight in a file that gets opened on a
# projector or read over a shoulder at a scoring table. Keeping the file off
# shared drives is what actually protects it.
#
# Leave it out entirely and no password is asked for.
#
#     ADMIN_PASSWORD_B64=cGFzc3dvcmQ=      # "password", as an example only

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the keys

# Every one of these is free except the AI one, and every one is optional except
# Nexus. The admin panel writes them here when you paste them, checks them on
# the way in, and TEST KEYS there is the only thing that says whether one works.
# A key hand-edited into this file needs the hub restarted to be picked up.
#
# Nexus - frc.nexus/api. The one worth stopping for: its "on field" is what arms
# the scouting screen on all six phones.
#
#     NEXUS_API_KEY=...
#
# Only if your team registered a push webhook with Nexus. The hub polls
# perfectly well without one, and rejects webhook posts that do not carry this.
#
#     NEXUS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=...
#
# The Blue Alliance - thebluealliance.com/account, "Read API Keys". Official
# results and per-robot climb; also the only source the fuel numbers come from.
#
#     TBA_API_KEY=...
#
# FRC Events - frc-events.firstinspires.org/services/API. Two halves, because
# FIRST authenticates with a username *and* a token. It posts the official
# result a few minutes before TBA does, and nothing else.
#
#     FRC_EVENTS_USER=...
#     FRC_EVENTS_TOKEN=...
#
# Lovat - what other teams' scouts wrote about the same robots. No page issues
# one; it is a curl, and setup.md walks through it. Starts `lvt-`.
#
#     LOVAT_API_KEY=...
#
# The AI model's key, from whichever company makes the model picked in the admin
# panel (Anthropic, Google, OpenAI, or OpenRouter for any of them through one
# bill). The only line here that costs money - cents per answer, and only when
# somebody presses a button. Leave the model on `none` and this is never read.
#
#     AI_API_KEY=...

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the mirror

# Two different secrets on purpose: the push key is how the hub proves it is the
# hub (write), the view passcode is what a person types to read the site (read).
# See mirror/README.md.
#
# MIRROR_PUSH_KEY is read by both sides - by the mirror as the key it accepts,
# and by the hub as the key it pushes with - so on one laptop trying the mirror
# out, this single line is both halves. On a real host, prefer systemd's own
# Environment= lines over this file, and set the view passcode only there.
#
#     MIRROR_PUSH_KEY=the-long-generated-one
#     MIRROR_VIEW_PASSCODE=the-one-people-type
